// ============================================================================
//  Lightroom_Color_Mixer.dctl
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  A Lightroom-STYLE (not Lightroom-identical) photographic Colour Mixer for
//  DaVinci Resolve Studio.  It provides independent Hue / Saturation /
//  Luminance control over eight overlapping hue regions, plus global
//  Saturation, Vibrance and a set of protection controls.
//
//  Version : 1.0.0
//  License : MIT
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WHAT THIS IS
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  This is an independent implementation that approximates the *behaviour* of
//  Adobe Lightroom's Colour Mixer / HSL panel using standard colour-science
//  techniques.  No Adobe code, data or binary was used, inspected or reverse
//  engineered.  It is NOT numerically equivalent to Lightroom and must not be
//  described as such.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  COLOUR-SPACE ASSUMPTIONS  (read this before grading)
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Hue is not a physical quantity: the hue angle of a pixel depends on the RGB
//  encoding it is measured in.  "Orange skin at ~30 degrees" is only true in a
//  perceptually encoded (gamma or log) RGB space.  In scene-linear RGB the very
//  same pixel measures a much lower hue angle and would be classified as red.
//  This DCTL therefore always evaluates hue in a perceptual encoding, and the
//  user must declare what is being fed to it with the "Input Encoding" control:
//
//    Display Rec.709 sRGB : input is display-referred and already perceptually
//                           encoded (Rec.709 / sRGB / Rec.1886 / P3 display,
//                           nominal 0-1).  The mixer runs directly on it.
//                           Luminance behaves like a display-referred tone
//                           gain with a highlight roll-off, so white stays
//                           white.
//
//    Log direct           : input is any log encoding (DaVinci Intermediate,
//                           ACEScct, LogC, S-Log3, V-Log, Log3G10, ...).  Log
//                           curves are already roughly perceptually uniform, so
//                           the mixer runs directly on the log code values and
//                           Luminance is applied as a code-value offset, which
//                           is a true scene exposure change (+/-1 stop at
//                           +/-100).
//
//    DaVinci WG Intermediate (default)
//                         : input is DaVinci Wide Gamut / DaVinci Intermediate,
//                           i.e. the output of a Colour Space Transform at the
//                           head of the node tree.  Processing is identical to
//                           Log direct - the mixer runs on the DaVinci WG values
//                           and nothing is ever clipped to a smaller gamut - but
//                           the hue angle used to CHOOSE the regions is measured
//                           after carrying the pixel into Rec.709, which is the
//                           space the grade is viewed in downstream of CST OUT.
//                           Without that step DaVinci WG's much wider primaries
//                           put a green leaf 13 degrees below where the eye puts
//                           it, so the Green slider would only half catch it.
//
//    Scene Linear         : input is scene-linear.  It is encoded to DaVinci
//                           Intermediate for processing and decoded back
//                           afterwards, so hue selection stays perceptual and
//                           Luminance is again a true exposure change.
//
//  Pixel VALUES are never converted between primaries: every operator runs on
//  the values you feed in, so nothing is ever clipped to a smaller gamut.  The
//  one place primaries are accounted for is the DaVinci WG mode, which measures
//  the hue angle used to CHOOSE the regions in Rec.709 - selection only, never
//  the pixel.  In the other three modes hue angles are read in whatever
//  primaries arrive: with Rec.709 or P3 the eight named regions land where you
//  expect, with very wide encodings such as ACES AP0 they do not, and the fix
//  there is a Colour Space Transform in front of the node and its inverse
//  behind it.
//
//  Luma is measured with Rec.709 weights (0.2126 / 0.7152 / 0.0722) in the
//  working encoding.  For other primaries this is an approximation, used only
//  as a lightness metric - it never leaves the pixel's own colour space.
//
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  ALGORITHM OVERVIEW
//  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1.  Optional transfer-function encode to the perceptual working domain.
//  2.  Measure max / min / chroma / hue / HSV-style saturation / luma.
//  3.  Hue is mapped onto a partition of unity built from eight region centres
//      (0 / 30 / 60 / 120 / 180 / 240 / 285 / 330 degrees).  Between two
//      adjacent centres the two regions cross-fade with a shaped, C1-continuous
//      blend; the eight weights always sum to exactly 1, so there are no hard
//      thresholds, no discontinuities and no banding.  A pixel sitting exactly
//      on a centre is controlled by that region alone, exactly as in a
//      photographic HSL panel.
//  4.  The eight (hue, sat, lum) slider triplets are blended by those weights
//      into a single effective triplet, which is then applied once.  Applying
//      one interpolated operator instead of eight stacked operators removes all
//      ordering artefacts and is what keeps gradients clean.
//  5.  Operators:
//        Hue        - rotation in HSV geometry: max/min (hence value and
//                     saturation) are held, only the hue angle moves.
//        Saturation - chroma scaling about the pixel's own luma.  This is
//                     exactly luma preserving by construction and reaches a
//                     true neutral at -100.
//        Luminance  - display domain: a hue and saturation preserving tone
//                     response that fixes both black and white.  Log domain: a
//                     code-value offset, which is an exposure change.  Linear
//                     domain: decode, scale, re-encode - an exact exposure
//                     change.
//  6.  Neutral protection, luminance preservation and a soft saturation /
//      gamut protector.
//  7.  Decode back to the input encoding and cross-fade by Overall Amount.
//
//  All operators are no-ops at their neutral setting, and every operator is
//  skipped by an explicit test at its neutral value, so the identity case is
//  bit-exact, not just close.
// ============================================================================


// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  UI
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

